{"id":20,"course_id":9,"title":"The Read-Only First Response Loop","summary":"Use get, describe, logs, and events before making changes.","sequence":1,"body_simplified":"## Debugging loop\nGood Kubernetes debugging starts with observation. The safe loop is: list the object, describe the object, read logs, inspect events, then form a hypothesis.\n- `kubectl get` shows current state and names.\n- `kubectl describe` shows status, events, mounts, probes, and scheduling details.\n- `kubectl logs` shows application output.\n- Events explain what Kubernetes attempted and why it failed.\n\n## Commands to practice\n$ kubectl get pods -n payments -o wide\n$ kubectl describe pod checkout-abc123 -n payments\n$ kubectl logs checkout-abc123 -n payments --since=15m\n$ kubectl get events -n payments --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nCapture evidence before deleting Pods or restarting Deployments. Restarts can destroy useful previous logs.\n\n## Signal to inspect\nLook for Status, Ready, Restarts, Last State, Events, node placement, image name, service account, and volume mount failures.","body_traditional":"## Debugging loop\nGood Kubernetes debugging starts with observation. The safe loop is: list the object, describe the object, read logs, inspect events, then form a hypothesis.\n- `kubectl get` shows current state and names.\n- `kubectl describe` shows status, events, mounts, probes, and scheduling details.\n- `kubectl logs` shows application output.\n- Events explain what Kubernetes attempted and why it failed.\n\n## Commands to practice\n$ kubectl get pods -n payments -o wide\n$ kubectl describe pod checkout-abc123 -n payments\n$ kubectl logs checkout-abc123 -n payments --since=15m\n$ kubectl get events -n payments --sort-by=.lastTimestamp\nCapture evidence before deleting Pods or restarting Deployments. Restarts can destroy useful previous logs.\n\n## Signal to inspect\nLook for Status, Ready, Restarts, Last State, Events, node placement, image name, service account, and volume mount failures.","pinyin":"Scenario: An app is unhealthy and someone wants to restart it immediately.\nChecklist:\n- Save `describe pod` output.\n- Save recent and previous logs if restarts happened.\n- Save namespace events sorted by time.\n- Only restart after you understand whether restart is a mitigation or evidence loss.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl logs checkout-abc123 -n payments --previous\n$ kubectl get events -n payments --sort-by=.lastTimestamp","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-kubectl-debugging-basics","course_category":"kubectl","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":71,"simplified":"kubectl get","traditional":"kubectl get","pinyin":"List","definition":"Shows object names and high-level status."},{"id":72,"simplified":"kubectl describe","traditional":"kubectl describe","pinyin":"Inspect","definition":"Shows detailed object state and events."},{"id":73,"simplified":"Event","traditional":"Event","pinyin":"Cluster signal","definition":"A Kubernetes record explaining scheduling, pulling, probing, or controller activity."},{"id":74,"simplified":"Previous logs","traditional":"Previous logs","pinyin":"Crash evidence","definition":"Logs from a previously terminated container instance."},{"id":75,"simplified":"Read-only diagnosis","traditional":"Read-only diagnosis","pinyin":"Safe workflow","definition":"Collecting evidence before making mutating changes."}],"flashcards":[{"id":43,"lesson_id":20,"prompt":"What four commands form the beginner debug loop?","answer":"get, describe, logs, and get events.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":44,"lesson_id":20,"prompt":"Why collect previous logs before restarting?","answer":"The evidence from the crashed container may disappear after another restart.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":45,"lesson_id":20,"prompt":"What do Kubernetes events usually explain?","answer":"Scheduling, image pulls, volume mounts, probe failures, and controller actions.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
